  "rationale": "Ellington's twelve-part Shakespeare suite is a defining statement of the Ellington–Strayhorn partnership and the load-bearing link between the band's swing-era past and its late-career extended works — the suite form that reaches directly forward to The Far East Suite (1966), already staged in this canon. Consensus is strong across independent listing traditions: NPR named it to the Basic Jazz Record Library (S6); the Penguin Guide to Jazz Recordings rates it four stars out of four (S1, Cook & Morton 2010); the Rolling Stone Jazz Record Guide gives it five stars (S1); AllMusic 4.5 stars (S2, Yanow). Session gravity is total — the full 1956–57 orchestra, a personnel network that is itself the big-band canon: Hodges, Gonsalves, Carney, Hamilton, Procope, Clark Terry, Ray Nance, Cat Anderson, Britt Woodman, Quentin Jackson, Jimmy Woode, Sam Woodyard. Lineage: 'The Star-Crossed Lovers' became a standard, and the suite is among the most studied and re-recorded Ellington works of the period. It still plays: witty, colorful, swinging writing with documented per-track solo features. The canon's Ellington holdings lean toward the trio (Money Jungle) and the 1960s orchestra (Far East Suite); this is the missing mid-1950s orchestral peak.",
