    "notes": "A twelve-part suite recorded in multiple sessions spanning August 7, 1956 to May 3, 1957 (S1; S2 confirms the same span), all at Columbia's 30th Street Studio, New York City per Irving Townsend's own liner notes (S3). Original release Columbia CL 1033 (S3). Producer Irving Townsend (S1, S3). Engineer not named in any consulted source — recorded null, epistemic unk. All twelve titles composed by Duke Ellington and Billy Strayhorn; Strayhorn is orchestrator/co-composer, not an instrumentalist, so he is not listed as a performer. The full orchestra plays throughout; featured solos rotate per track (Townsend's liner notes, S3): the title track features the ensemble; 'Sonnet in Search of a Moor' features Jimmy Woode (bass); 'Up and Down' features Jimmy Hamilton (clarinet) and Ray Nance (violin) as the lovers, with Clark Terry (trumpet) as Puck; 'Sonnet for Sister Kate' features Quentin Jackson (trombone, plunger); 'The Star-Crossed Lovers' features Johnny Hodges (alto) and Paul Gonsalves (tenor); 'Madness in Great Ones' features Cat Anderson (trumpet); 'Half the Fun' features Johnny Hodges; 'Circle of Fourths' features Paul Gonsalves. Ray Nance's violin and John Sanders's valve trombone are noted as instrument doubles/variants. The 1999 Columbia/Legacy reissue added ten bonus tracks (stereo masters, alternate takes, and outtakes) — excluded from the track list here per the original-release convention (S1).",
