    "tier": "consensus_core",
    "priority": "strong",
    "case_for": "Four independent listing traditions — NPR's Basic Jazz Record Library (S6), the Penguin Guide's 4/4 (Cook & Morton, via S1), the Rolling Stone Jazz Record Guide's 5★ (S1), and AllMusic's 4.5★ (S2) — converge at or near top tier, the strongest single signal the rubric names. It is the definitive mid-career Ellington–Strayhorn suite, the structural template that leads directly forward to the already-staged Far East Suite (1966), and 'The Star-Crossed Lovers' entered the standard repertoire. Session gravity is total: the full 1956–57 orchestra — Hodges, Gonsalves, Carney, Clark Terry, Nance, Hamilton, Procope — is itself the post-war big-band canon, and the Shakespeare conceit frames each soloist in witty, swinging, cold-listen-accessible writing.",
    "case_against": "The honest against-case is that Ellington's 1950s suites run parallel to the post-bop tradition rather than inside its central small-group artery (Davis, Blakey, Silver, Monk); a strict purist could call this a masterpiece of American music that nonetheless doesn't teach the hard-bop/modal vocabulary the canon's center of gravity is built on. The sourcing is also listing-tradition-heavy — Penguin and Rolling Stone ratings are relayed through Wikipedia (S1) rather than cited directly, and no DownBeat, JazzTimes, or academic critical essay appears — so the consensus signal is real but qualitatively thinner than its quantity suggests.",
    "disagreement": "Both references agree on consensus_core; they split on priority — Ref 1 argues must_have on the gap-filling logic (the canon holds Money Jungle 1962 and Far East Suite 1966 but no mid-1950s orchestral apex), while Ref 2 settles at strong, treating the parallel-to-post-bop concern as enough to keep it off the must_have tier.",
    "recommendation": "The council proposes John include this as consensus_core at strong priority, weighing the overwhelming cross-tradition consensus and direct lineage to Far East Suite against the parallel-tradition concern that kept one voice off must_have.",
